{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546525302,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb172a1ffej1jOZ3ysNoYkNr","part":{"partID":"prt_1d34ee227001pB5zr31Xr8u2f4","sessionID":"ses_e2cb172a1ffej1jOZ3ysNoYkNr","messageID":"msg_1d34ebf1b001qZDR6RYqecXzRd","type":"tool","id":"call_3743757611c94e968294d36a","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/plugins/README.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/plugins/README.md, lines 1-257\n1: ---\n2: title: \"OpenCode Plugin Entrypoints\"\n3: description: \"JavaScript plugin entrypoints for context injection, lifecycle handling, policy guards, runtime tools and post-action checks. OpenCode discovers these via a flat glob over .opencode/plugins/, and each module default-exports the object `{ id, setup, server }`.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"OpenCode plugins\"\n6:   - \"plugin entrypoints\"\n7:   - \"OpenCode hook events\"\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # OpenCode Plugin Entrypoints\n11: \n12: > JavaScript plugin entrypoints for context injection, lifecycle handling, policy guards, runtime tools and post-action checks.\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n17: \n18: `.opencode/plugins/` contains the JavaScript modules OpenCode discovers as local plugins. Each module default-exports one plain object `{ id, setup, server }`: `setup` is the OpenCode 2 (recommended) entry and `server` the OpenCode 1 (fallback) entry. Source plugins build that object with `dualPlugin(id, server)` from `lib/v2-bridge.js`, so the v1 factory stays the single implementation and the bridge derives the v2 leg from it. OpenCode loads plugins by a flat glob over this folder (it does not recurse), so every loadable plugin lives directly here. The `lib/` and `tests/` subfolders are not plugin sources.\n19: \n20: Every plugin here runs on both OpenCode majors. OpenCode 2 calls `setup(ctx)` and OpenCode 1 calls `server(input)`, and neither host calls the other's entry, so a plugin that exported only one entry would do nothing on the other host. On 2026-10-19 the 17 plugin files then present were verified on OpenCode 2.0.32 and OpenCode 1.19.3: the doctor battery reported all 17 active on 2.0.32 and discovered all 17 on 1.19.3. On 2026-10-20 a live run of every plugin showed each one's effect on both versions. One of those plugins has since been removed, and `decision-capture.js` was added later through the same bridge without a rerun of that battery. `session-history-lookup.js` was added the same way, and so was `env-secret-guard.js`, which has no live battery run yet; on 2026-11-08 a live OpenCode 2.2.5 session showed its section and redeemed the printed command, and a live OpenCode 1.19.4 session did the same. `overlay-guard.js` was added the same way and has no live battery run yet. [ARCHITECTURE.md](./ARCHITECTURE.md) explains the dual export, the bridge and what has no OpenCode 2 equivalent.\n21: \n22: <!-- readme-check\n23: allow-count 17 plugin files | a dated count of the files present at the 2026-10-19 battery, not the current folder\n24: allow-count 17 plugins | a dated count of the files present at the 2026-10-19 battery, not the current folder\n25: -->\n26: \n27: The directory inventory is authoritative for the auto-loaded plugin surface. Shared policy cores stay under their owning skill (`system-spec-kit`, `system-skill-advisor`, `sk-git`, `sk-code`, `system-completion`, `system-deep-loop`). Most files here are **transport adapters** that translate OpenCode events into those cores and keep terminal output out of the TUI. `opencode-goal.js` is the exception: it implements the goal state machine and goal tools itself and shares only selected helpers. The single shared boundary every plugin honors in normal operation: **never write to stdout or stderr**, because OpenCode overlays those onto the TUI prompt line and corrupts the interactive session. Findings are injected via `experimental.chat.system.transform`, returned from tool handlers or persisted to bounded workspace logs.\n28: \n29: Most plugins honor a per-concern kill switch through the shared `hook-flags.cjs` resolver (`isHookEnabled('<concern>')`), plus the master `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED`. A disabled plugin stops its main work.\n30: \n31: Most become full no-ops, while the goal tools and the advisor status tool stay registered and report that they are disabled. All advisory checks fail open.\n32: \n33: Four plugins reject by default by throwing from `tool.execute.before`: `sk-git-message-gate.js`, the dispatch guard in `cli-dispatch-audit.js`, `env-secret-guard.js`, which refuses a read or write of a dotenv file, and `overlay-guard.js`, which refuses a write to a file a vendored tree's overlay owns. Other rejection is opt-in: `SYSTEM_SPEC_GATE_ENFORCE` for spec-gate deny, and `SYSTEM_DEEP_LOOP_GUARD_REJECT=1` or `SYSTEM_DEEP_LOOP_GUARD_REJECT_LOOP=1` for the deep-loop guard, which otherwise only warns.\n34: \n35: One plugin is an exception. `sk-git-message-gate.js` has no switch, because the commit, PR and branch rules carry no bypass on any surface.\n36: \n37: ---\n38: \n39: ## 2. WHAT'S HERE / INVENTORY\n40: \n41: | File | Concern | Hook surface | Responsibility |\n42: |---|---|---|---|\n43: | `ask-surface-guard.js` | `ask-surface` | `event` (`session.idle`) | When a turn ends in a typed option menu and called no `question` tool, sends the turn back once through `session.promptAsync` with an instruction to ask through `question`. Writes nothing to stdout or stderr, fails open, and stays off under `SYSTEM_ASK_SURFACE_DISABLED=1`. |\n44: | `classifier-injection-screen.js` | `injection-screen` | `tool.execute.after` for `webfetch`, `experimental.chat.system.transform` | Screens fetched `webfetch` text for instructions aimed at the agent. Buffers up to 20 advisories per session and drains them on the next transform, the model call that reads the fetch result. Never writes stdout/stderr. |\n45: | `cli-dispatch-audit.js` | `dispatch`, `dispatch-guard` | `tool.execute.before` and `tool.execute.after` for Bash | `tool.execute.before` blocks a CLI dispatch that breaks a hard rule declared in the target skill's `SKILL.md` by throwing, and fails open on its own errors. `tool.execute.after` records redacted dispatch telemetry after completed CLI calls, recognizes an `opencode` or `opencode2` invocation and appends a bounded audit record. |\n46: | `codex-hooks-watchdog.js` | `codex-watchdog` | `event` (`session.created`) | Watches Codex hook installation and reports drift. Never affects the guarded session. |\n47: | `decision-capture.js` | `decision-capture` | `tool.execute.after` for `question`, `experimental.chat.system.transform` | Queues each answered `question` call for the next full-auto save under the same key as the save-time sweep, and pushes one system line when the count of answers still waiting for a decision record rises. Fails open and never changes the tool output. |\n48: | `env-secret-guard.js` | `env-secret-guard` | `tool.execute.before`, `tool` (`env_secret_list_keys`, `env_secret_validate`, `env_secret_fingerprint`) | Refuses a file tool or shell command that would read or write a dotenv file by throwing, through the shared core in `../hooks/env-secret-guard/`. Its three tools list keys, check syntax and fingerprint one value without returning any value. Fails open on its own errors. |\n49: | `mcp-route-guard.js` | `mcp-route-guard` | `tool.execute.before` | Advises when native MCP calls should use Code Mode. Persists to a bounded, rotated workspace log. |\n50: | `overlay-guard.js` | `overlay-guard` | `tool.execute.before` for write tools | Refuses a write tool that names a file a vendored tree's overlay owns, by throwing, through the shared core in `../hooks/overlay-guard/`. The reason names the overlay file to edit instead. A write it cannot judge is allowed, and its notes go to a bounded, rotated log because OpenCode paints stdout and stderr onto the prompt line. |\n51: | `opencode-goal.js` | `goal` | `event` (`session.created`/`status`/`idle`/`deleted`, `message.updated`/`message.part.updated`, `permission.asked`/`replied`, `question.asked`/`replied`/`rejected`), `experimental.chat.system.transform`, `tool` (goal tools) | Holds a text goal or binds a session to a packet `goal.md` and injects the objective slice built from it (via `../hooks/goal/lib/goal-slice.cjs`). Largest plugin in this folder. Owns the goal state machine, supervisor, continuation, capabilities and the goal tool surface including `bind`, `resent` and `packet`. |\n52: | `session-cleanup.js` | `session-cleanup` | `event` (`session.created`/`deleted`), `experimental.chat.system.transform`, `dispose` | Performs bounded session and host cleanup. Startup guards and teardown cleanup. Reaps MCP helper processes via `session-cleanup.sh`. |\n53: | `session-history-lookup.js` | `session-lifecycle` | `event` (`session.compacted`), `experimental.chat.system.transform` | On `session.compacted`, saves a history locator keyed by the checkout root above the session folder, then pushes the shared `## History Lookup` section into each request's system array while the locator is under 30 minutes old. Fails open: no build, no checkout or a failed save means no section. |\n54: | `session-relaunch.js` | `session-relaunch` | `event` (`message.updated`/`message.part.updated`/`session.idle`), `experimental.chat.system.transform` | Tracks usage and seed text. Inside `skilled-loop`, while this session has no matching relaunch record, it pushes the active nudge on every request. Outside the loop the nudge asks for a note and `keep` instead of `prepare`, because only the wrapper can relaunch the session. While a matching seed's note is open, it injects the seed on each request, then a claimed reminder once the note is claimed. It arms the record on `session.idle`. The plugin runs the detached killer with `node` because OpenCode hosts plugins on Bun. |\n55: | `sk-code-post-edit-quality.js` | `post-edit-quality` | `tool.execute.before`, `tool.execute.after`, `experimental.chat.system.transform` | Runs bounded post-edit quality checks. `tool.execute.before` stashes the edited path keyed by callID, because the after hook has no file path. `tool.execute.after` runs the router core. Findings drain on the next transform and are recorded to a workspace log. |\n56: | `sk-git-message-gate.js` | `git-message-gate` (no kill switch) | `tool.execute.before` for `bash` | Refuses a `git commit` message, `gh pr` description or new branch name that breaks the repository's own sk-git templates by throwing. Allows what the shared gate cannot parse, but refuses when the repository's rules exist and cannot be read. It has no kill switch, because the commit and push rules carry no bypass on any surface. |\n57: | `sk-git-preflight-advisory.js` | `git-preflight` | `tool.execute.before` for `bash`, `experimental.chat.system.transform` | Advises on Git command scope before execution. Buffers at most 20 advisory events and drains them on the next transform. Never prints. |\n58: | `system-completion-sentinel.js` | `completion` | `event` (`session.created`/`idle`) | Checks completion evidence at session lifecycle points. On `session.idle`, resolves completion state via `ctx.client.session.messages()` (guarded, fail-open). Throttled-sweeps the shared state dir on `session.created`. |\n59: | `system-deep-loop-guard.js` | `task-dispatch` | `event` (`session.created`), `tool.execute.before` | Checks deep-loop Task dispatches and repeat handoffs. Sweeps stale loop-guard state on `session.created`. |\n60: | `system-dist-freshness-guard.js` | `dist-freshness` | `tool.execute.before`, `event` (`session.created`/`deleted`), `experimental.chat.system.transform` | Reports stale compiled outputs and invalidates diagnostics. Short-circuits the whole plugin at factory entry when disabled. |\n61: | `system-skill-advisor.js` | `skill-advisor` | `experimental.chat.system.transform`, `event`, `tool` (`spec_kit_skill_advisor_status`) | Injects skill-routing guidance per prompt and exposes advisor status. Spawns the advisor subprocess only on a cache miss, behind a TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup. |\n62: | `system-spec-gate.js` | `spec-gate` | `experimental.chat.system.transform`, `tool.execute.before`, `event` | Classifies and evaluates mutation-gate state. Classify best-effort fetches the last user message via `ctx.client`. Enforce throws `system-spec-gate:` on deny (OpenCode's deny signal). |\n63: | `system-speckit-completion.js` | `completion` | `tool` (read-only completion evidence) | Exposes read-only completion evidence. Checks the `completion` concern at factory entry. |\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: ## 3. SHARED LIB MODULES\n68: \n69: `lib/` holds the modules the entry files import. None of them is a plugin source, and the loader never registers one on its own.\n70: \n71: | File | Responsibility |\n72: |---|---|\n73: | `lib/v2-bridge.js` | Builds the dual export. `dualPlugin(id, server)` returns `{ id, setup, server }`: `setup` maps its hooks (event stream, `tool.execute.before`/`after`, `experimental.chat.system.transform`, tool definitions) onto OpenCode 2 registrations and returns a cleanup function, while `server` stays the unchanged OpenCode 1 factory. It reports v2 `shell` and `subagent` calls under their v1 names, announces each session once with `session.created`, and fills the message events v1 plugins read. Hook keys the bridge does not map are reported through `cleanup.skippedKeys`. |\n74: | `lib/tool-identity.js` | Identity helper for tool definitions. `tool(definition)` returns the definition unchanged, and `tool.schema` is zod, so one definition serves the v1 `tool` hook and the bridge's v2 tool registration. |\n75: | `lib/opencode-message-identity.js` | Stable transform identity and dedup state. `system-skill-advisor.js` uses it when `deduplicateTransforms` is on. |\n76: | `lib/skill-advisor-render.js` | Advisor fallback and compiled-route renderers, shared by the advisor plugin and its tests. |\n77: | `lib/classifier-injection-screen-core.js` | Constants and the session-id resolver for the injection-screen plugin. |\n78: | `lib/completion-sentinel-core.js` | Re-export of the completion-evidence sentinel core under `skills/system-spec-kit`. |\n79: | `lib/opencode-goal-core.js` | WeakMap registry that binds a goal plugin module's helpers to its server, so both entries read the same state. |\n80: | `lib/sk-code-post-edit-quality-core.js` | Constants, the callID-to-filePath correlation map and the finding formatter for the post-edit-quality plugin. |\n81: \n82: The bridge mapping is exercised against stub contexts in the test suites. On 2026-10-19 the doctor battery confirmed that all 17 plugins load on 1.19.3 and show active on 2.0.32. On 2026-10-20 a probe plugin on a private OpenCode 2.0.32 host received `session.created` and `session.deleted` under those names for sessions created and removed through the server API, and received `location.shutdown` instead of `server.instance.disposed` or `global.disposed`. The probe did not start a session with `opencode run`, and v2 can deliver session traffic before `session.created`, so the bridge synthesizes a `session.created` the first time it sees traffic for a session and drops a later duplicate (`specs/cli-external-orchestration/opencode-platform/002-opencode-v1-v2-dual-compat/024-plugin-inventory-guards/scratch/v2-events/README.txt`). `tests/plugin-inventory.json` lists every event name a bridged plugin handles that the bridge neither maps nor aliases, grouped as observed, not delivered or unverified. The `plugin-utilization` playbook in `cli-opencode` checks each plugin's effect in a live session; on 2026-10-20 all 17 acted on OpenCode 1.19.3 and 2.0.32.\n83: \n84: ---\n85: \n86: ## 4. TEST SUITES\n87: \n88: `tests/` holds the regression suites for the entry files. They run on the Node built-in test runner with hermetic stubs. The v2 suites are:\n89: \n90: | File | Covers |\n91: |---|---|\n92: | `tests/v2-bridge.test.cjs` | The bridge itself: event aliases and mappings, the v1 client adapters, hook registration and cleanup, tool result conversion and the `tool` identity contract. |\n93: | `tests/v2-hard-plugins.test.cjs` | Selected hard cases, not the plugin list: expected v2 registrations for `system-completion-sentinel`, `system-spec-gate`, `system-skill-advisor` and `opencode-goal` through a stub v2 context, plus behavior cases for the first four. It also requires a case for every native-dual exception in the inventory. |\n94: | `tests/v2-registration.test.cjs` | The dual export shape, at least one v2 registration and the cleanup function for every bridged plugin the inventory finds. |\n95: | `tests/plugin-inventory.test.cjs` | The inventory itself: every `.js` file is bridged or a listed native-dual exception, every bridged plugin maps all its hooks, and every event name a bridged plugin compares against is mapped, aliased or listed in `tests/plugin-inventory.json`. The event names come from parsing each plugin's `type` comparisons, `switch` statements on the event type and literal collections; any other kind of event check, such as `eventType.endsWith('.disposed')`, must be listed under `unscannedEventChecks` with a reason. |\n96: \n97: The plugin list comes from the folder, not from a hand-written list. `tests/helpers/plugin-inventory.cjs` treats a file as bridged when its source contains `dualPlugin('<file stem>'`, and `tests/v2-registration.test.cjs` then checks that its default export has the dual shape, and `tests/plugin-inventory.json` names the exceptions, with an empty `nativeDual` list. A new plugin file that is neither bridged nor listed fails `tests/plugin-inventory.test.cjs`.\n98: \n99: The remaining `*.test.cjs` files cover v1 behavior and adjacent contracts. `tests/README.md` indexes every suite, and `tests/helpers/` holds the shared fixtures.\n100: \n101: ---\n102: \n103: ## 5. DIRECTORY TREE\n104: \n105: ```text\n106: plugins/\n107: +-- README.md                              # this index\n108: +-- ARCHITECTURE.md                        # dual-version design, bridge and limits\n109: +-- ask-surface-guard.js\n110: +-- classifier-injection-screen.js\n111: +-- cli-dispatch-audit.js\n112: +-- codex-hooks-watchdog.js\n113: +-- decision-capture.js\n114: +-- env-secret-guard.js\n115: +-- mcp-route-guard.js\n116: +-- overlay-guard.js\n117: +-- opencode-goal.js\n118: +-- session-cleanup.js\n119: +-- session-history-lookup.js\n120: +-- session-relaunch.js\n121: +-- sk-code-post-edit-quality.js\n122: +-- sk-git-message-gate.js\n123: +-- sk-git-preflight-advisory.js\n124: +-- system-completion-sentinel.js\n125: +-- system-deep-loop-guard.js\n126: +-- system-dist-freshness-guard.js\n127: +-- system-skill-advisor.js\n128: +-- system-spec-gate.js\n129: +-- system-speckit-completion.js\n130: +-- lib/\n131: |   +-- classifier-injection-screen-core.js\n132: |   +-- completion-sentinel-core.js\n133: |   +-- opencode-goal-core.js\n134: |   +-- opencode-message-identity.js\n135: |   +-- sk-code-post-edit-quality-core.js\n136: |   +-- skill-advisor-render.js\n137: |   +-- tool-identity.js\n138: |   `-- v2-bridge.js\n139: `-- tests/\n140:     +-- README.md\n141:     +-- helpers/\n142:     +-- plugin-inventory.test.cjs\n143:     +-- plugin-inventory.json\n144:     +-- v2-bridge.test.cjs\n145:     +-- v2-hard-plugins.test.cjs\n146:     +-- v2-registration.test.cjs\n147:     `-- *.test.cjs                         # the remaining plugin suites\n148: ```\n149: \n150: ---\n151: \n152: ## 6. KEY FILES\n153: \n154: | File | Responsibility |\n155: |---|---|\n156: | `opencode-goal.js` | The largest plugin. Owns the goal state machine, supervisor, continuation, capabilities and the goal tool surface. Hooks session, message, permission and question events, `experimental.chat.system.transform` and a family of goal tools. |\n157: | `system-skill-advisor.js` | Spawns the skill-advisor subprocess on a prompt-cache miss, manages a TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup, integrates transform-dedup where the opt-in same-message dedup bases its decision on the full advisor block before directive-lifecycle reduction and suppresses a repeated transform for the same message and exposes `spec_kit_skill_advisor_status`. |\n158: | `system-spec-gate.js` | Maps OpenCode's transport onto the runtime-neutral spec-gate core. Classify best-effort fetches the last user message via `ctx.client`. Enforce throws `system-spec-gate:` on deny. `event` sweeps, advances and evicts state. |\n159: | `sk-code-post-edit-quality.js` | Correlates `tool.execute.before` file paths to `tool.execute.after` callIDs, runs the post-edit router core and drains findings on the next transform. |\n160: | `lib/v2-bridge.js` | Builds the dual export for every bridged plugin. `server` is the unchanged v1 factory, and `setup` maps its hooks onto OpenCode 2 registrations and returns a cleanup function. |\n161: | `lib/tool-identity.js` | The `tool` identity helper. Returns a v1 tool definition unchanged and carries zod as `tool.schema`. |\n162: | `lib/opencode-message-identity.js` | Shared stable transform identity and dedup state used by the advisor plugin. |\n163: \n164: ---\n165: \n166: ## 7. CONFIGURATION\n167: \n168: Every plugin except `session-relaunch.js` is enabled by default. Truthy disable values are `1`, `true`, `yes` and `on` (case-insensitive) for the shared resolver. Each plugin that honors a switch checks `isHookEnabled('<concern>')` at entry (some at factory entry, some per-hook) and stops its main work when disabled. Housekeeping can still run, such as the deep-loop guard's stale-state sweep on `session.created`. The one exception named in the overview does not.\n169: \n170: | Variable | Effect |\n171: |---|---|\n172: | `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED=1` | Master switch that disables every plugin in this folder that reads `hook-flags.cjs`, along with every other repo hook. It does not reach `sk-git-message-gate.js`. |\n173: | Per-concern switch | The default name is `SYSTEM_<CONCERN>_DISABLED` (e.g. `SYSTEM_SPEC_GATE_DISABLED`, `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED`). Six concerns use another canonical name: `OPENCODE_GOAL_DISABLED`, `CLI_DISPATCH_AUDIT_DISABLED`, `MCP_ROUTE_GUARD_DISABLED`, `CODEX_HOOKS_WATCHDOG_DISABLED`, `SK_GIT_PREFLIGHT_DISABLED` and `SK_CODE_POST_EDIT_QUALITY_DISABLED`. |\n174: | Older aliases | `hook-flags.cjs` still honors older names, e.g. `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `OPENCODE_GOAL_PLUGIN_DISABLED`. See each plugin's own README. |\n175: | `SYSTEM_SESSION_RELAUNCH_OPENCODE_ENABLED`, `SYSTEM_SESSION_RELAUNCH_ENABLED` | Turn on `session-relaunch.js`, which ships off. A non-empty OpenCode flag decides for OpenCode (`1` on, `0` off), and the global flag decides otherwise. `SYSTEM_SESSION_RELAUNCH_DISABLED=1` and the master switch still win. Also read from `.skilled/hooks/hook-flags.env`. |\n176: \n177: Optional tuning: `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` (default 3000, owned by `system-skill-advisor`), `SYSTEM_OPENCODE_TRANSFORM_DEDUP=1` (opt-in transform dedup for the advisor plugin), plus per-plugin cache/budget/timeout envs documented in each plugin's README.\n178: \n179: Set a flag inline for one command, export it for a session or persist it in `.skilled/hooks/hook-flags.env` (copied from `hook-flags.env.example`, gitignored). The environment always wins over the file, so a persisted default can be overridden for a single session. Only switches read through `hook-flags.cjs` can be persisted there.\n180: \n181: ---\n182: \n183: ## 8. BOUNDARIES AND FLOW\n184: \n185: | Boundary | Rule |\n186: |---|---|\n187: | Transport only | Plugin files own the OpenCode transport boundary. Shared policy and runtime-neutral logic belongs under the owning skill. |\n188: | Dual export | Every plugin file default-exports one plain object `{ id, setup, server }`: `setup` is the OpenCode 2 (recommended) entry and `server` the OpenCode 1 (fallback) entry. `lib/` modules are shared helpers, never plugin sources. |\n189: | No stdout/stderr | Plugins do not print to stdout or stderr in normal operation. The only writes are opt-in debug output, such as the goal plugin's debug env. OpenCode overlays those onto the TUI prompt line and corrupts the interactive session. Findings go through `experimental.chat.system.transform`, tool returns or bounded workspace logs. |\n190: | Fail-open | All advisory checks fail open. A missing payload, a subprocess timeout, a parse failure or any internal error resolves to a no-op, or for the read-only completion tool to a payload that marks the missing sections unavailable. The skill advisor is the exception to the no-op because on a subprocess timeout, a parse failure, a missing prompt or an internal error it injects its status-headed fallback (an `Advisor: outage (...)` or `Advisor: prompt skipped.` line above the directives block) and never blocks the prompt. The git message gate and the dispatch guard reject by default. Spec-gate rejection is opt-in through `SYSTEM_SPEC_GATE_ENFORCE`, and deep-loop guard rejection through `SYSTEM_DEEP_LOOP_GUARD_REJECT=1` or `SYSTEM_DEEP_LOOP_GUARD_REJECT_LOOP=1`. |\n191: | Kill-switch | Every plugin except the one named in the overview honors its per-concern kill-switch via the shared `hook-flags.cjs` resolver plus the master `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED`. A disabled plugin stops its main work, and the goal and advisor status tools stay registered to report that. Housekeeping such as the deep-loop guard's stale-state sweep can still run. |\n192: | Bounded | Subprocess spawns, pending-event buffers and workspace logs are bounded, as is the skill advisor's TTL+LRU prompt cache (per-plugin limits documented in each plugin's README). The advisor's workspace-surface cache instead keeps one entry per workspace directory the process has seen, so it is not capped. |\n193: | Tests | Plugin tests stay under `tests/`. |\n194: \n195: ---\n196: \n197: ## 9. HOOK MODEL\n198: \n199: Plugin factories register some subset of:\n200: \n201: - `tool`: tools exposed to the model (e.g. `spec_kit_skill_advisor_status`, the goal tools, the read-only completion-evidence tool).\n202: - `tool.execute.before`: pre-tool evaluation (spec-gate enforce, dispatch guard, env-secret-guard, git message gate, git-preflight, mcp-route-guard, post-edit-quality path stash, deep-loop guard).\n203: - `tool.execute.after`: post-tool evaluation (cli-dispatch-audit, post-edit-quality run, injection screen).\n204: - `experimental.chat.system.transform`: per-turn system-context injection (advisor, spec-gate classify, goal, session-cleanup, session-relaunch nudge and seed, git-preflight drain, post-edit-quality drain, dist-freshness and injection-screen drain).\n205: - `event`: lifecycle and activity handlers (`session.created`/`status`/`idle`/`deleted`/`resumed`/`compacted`/`compact`, `message.updated`/`message.part.updated`, the goal plugin's permission and question events, `server.instance.disposed`/`global.disposed`).\n206: - `dispose`: teardown when the host unloads the plugin (session-cleanup).\n207: \n208: The bridge in `lib/v2-bridge.js` maps that set onto OpenCode 2 registrations: `event` to the event stream, `tool.execute.before`/`after` to the tool execute hooks, `experimental.chat.system.transform` to the session context hook and `tool` definitions to the tool transform. A key outside the set is reported through `cleanup.skippedKeys`.\n209: \n210: ---\n211: \n212: ## 10. VALIDATION\n213: \n214: Run the Node regression command from the repository root:\n215: \n216: ```bash\n217: node --test .opencode/plugins/tests/*.test.cjs\n218: ```\n219: \n220: Expected result: Node discovers every current CJS test file and reports the suite result. A failing test is a validation failure.\n221: \n222: ```bash\n223: (\n224:   rc=0\n225:   for f in .opencode/plugins/*.js\n226:   do node --check \"$f\" || rc=1\n227:   done\n228:   exit \"$rc\"\n229: )\n230: ```\n231: \n232: Expected result: exit status 0, with no syntax errors across every plugin. Any failing file makes the command exit 1. `node --check` checks syntax only and does not resolve imports.\n233: \n234: Check both OpenCode versions with the doctor battery:\n235: \n236: ```bash\n237: node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --family=v1\n238: node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --family=v2\n239: ```\n240: \n241: Expected result: each exits 0, reporting all plugin files active on OpenCode 2 (recommended) and all discovered on OpenCode 1 (fallback). The v2 run starts its own private server.\n242: \n243: ---\n244: \n245: ## 11. RELATED\n246: \n247: - [`ARCHITECTURE.md`](./ARCHITECTURE.md): the dual-version design, the bridge, the kill switches and the known limits.\n248: - [`tests/README.md`](./tests/README.md): the plugin regression suites.\n249: - [`tests/helpers/README.md`](./tests/helpers/README.md): the shared test helpers.\n250: - [`../hooks/README.md`](../hooks/README.md): the unified hooks tree with the kill-switch index and coverage matrix. Plugins are mirrored there as the OpenCode adapter for their concern (`spec-gate`, `skill-advisor`, `git-preflight`, `git-message-gate`, `session-cleanup`, `injection-screen`).\n251: - [`../skills/`](../skills/): the shared skill cores these plugins adapt.\n252: \n253: <!-- readme-check\n254: allow-count four plugins | the sentence counts the plugins that reject by default by behavior, not the plugin files in this folder\n255: allow-count 17 plugin files | the sentence reports the 2026-10-19 battery run and names the plugins added since\n256: allow-count 17 plugins | the sentence reports the 2026-10-19 battery run, not the current folder\n257: -->","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/plugins/README.md, lines 1-257\n1: ---\n2: title: \"OpenCode Plugin Entrypoints\"\n3: description: \"JavaScript plugin entrypoints for context injection, lifecycle handling, policy guards, runtime tools and post-action checks. OpenCode discovers these via a flat glob over .opencode/plugins/, and each module default-exports the object `{ id, setup, server }`.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"OpenCode plugins\"\n6:   - \"plugin entrypoints\"\n7:   - \"OpenCode hook events\"\n8: ---\n9: \n10: # OpenCode Plugin Entrypoints\n11: \n12: > JavaScript plugin entrypoints for context injection, lifecycle handling, policy guards, runtime tools and post-action checks.\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n17: \n18: `.opencode/plugins/` contains the JavaScript modules OpenCode discovers as local plugins. Each module default-exports one plain object `{ id, setup, server }`: `setup` is the OpenCode 2 (recommended) entry and `server` the OpenCode 1 (fallback) entry. Source plugins build that object with `dualPlugin(id, server)` from `lib/v2-bridge.js`, so the v1 factory stays the single implementation and the bridge derives the v2 leg from it. OpenCode loads plugins by a flat glob over this folder (it does not recurse), so every loadable plugin lives directly here. The `lib/` and `tests/` subfolders are not plugin sources.\n19: \n20: Every plugin here runs on both OpenCode majors. OpenCode 2 calls `setup(ctx)` and OpenCode 1 calls `server(input)`, and neither host calls the other's entry, so a plugin that exported only one entry would do nothing on the other host. On 2026-10-19 the 17 plugin files then present were verified on OpenCode 2.0.32 and OpenCode 1.19.3: the doctor battery reported all 17 active on 2.0.32 and discovered all 17 on 1.19.3. On 2026-10-20 a live run of every plugin showed each one's effect on both versions. One of those plugins has since been removed, and `decision-capture.js` was added later through the same bridge without a rerun of that battery. `session-history-lookup.js` was added the same way, and so was `env-secret-guard.js`, which has no live battery run yet; on 2026-11-08 a live OpenCode 2.2.5 session showed its section and redeemed the printed command, and a live OpenCode 1.19.4 session did the same. `overlay-guard.js` was added the same way and has no live battery run yet. [ARCHITECTURE.md](./ARCHITECTURE.md) explains the dual export, the bridge and what has no OpenCode 2 equivalent.\n21: \n22: <!-- readme-check\n23: allow-count 17 plugin files | a dated count of the files present at the 2026-10-19 battery, not the current folder\n24: allow-count 17 plugins | a dated count of the files present at the 2026-10-19 battery, not the current folder\n25: -->\n26: \n27: The directory inventory is authoritative for the auto-loaded plugin surface. Shared policy cores stay under their owning skill (`system-spec-kit`, `system-skill-advisor`, `sk-git`, `sk-code`, `system-completion`, `system-deep-loop`). Most files here are **transport adapters** that translate OpenCode events into those cores and keep terminal output out of the TUI. `opencode-goal.js` is the exception: it implements the goal state machine and goal tools itself and shares only selected helpers. The single shared boundary every plugin honors in normal operation: **never write to stdout or stderr**, because OpenCode overlays those onto the TUI prompt line and corrupts the interactive session. Findings are injected via `experimental.chat.system.transform`, returned from tool handlers or persisted to bounded workspace logs.\n28: \n29: Most plugins honor a per-concern kill switch through the shared `hook-flags.cjs` resolver (`isHookEnabled('<concern>')`), plus the master `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED`. A disabled plugin stops its main work.\n30: \n31: Most become full no-ops, while the goal tools and the advisor status tool stay registered and report that they are disabled. All advisory checks fail open.\n32: \n33: Four plugins reject by default by throwing from `tool.execute.before`: `sk-git-message-gate.js`, the dispatch guard in `cli-dispatch-audit.js`, `env-secret-guard.js`, which refuses a read or write of a dotenv file, and `overlay-guard.js`, which refuses a write to a file a vendored tree's overlay owns. Other rejection is opt-in: `SYSTEM_SPEC_GATE_ENFORCE` for spec-gate deny, and `SYSTEM_DEEP_LOOP_GUARD_REJECT=1` or `SYSTEM_DEEP_LOOP_GUARD_REJECT_LOOP=1` for the deep-loop guard, which otherwise only warns.\n34: \n35: One plugin is an exception. `sk-git-message-gate.js` has no switch, because the commit, PR and branch rules carry no bypass on any surface.\n36: \n37: ---\n38: \n39: ## 2. WHAT'S HERE / INVENTORY\n40: \n41: | File | Concern | Hook surface | Responsibility |\n42: |---|---|---|---|\n43: | `ask-surface-guard.js` | `ask-surface` | `event` (`session.idle`) | When a turn ends in a typed option menu and called no `question` tool, sends the turn back once through `session.promptAsync` with an instruction to ask through `question`. Writes nothing to stdout or stderr, fails open, and stays off under `SYSTEM_ASK_SURFACE_DISABLED=1`. |\n44: | `classifier-injection-screen.js` | `injection-screen` | `tool.execute.after` for `webfetch`, `experimental.chat.system.transform` | Screens fetched `webfetch` text for instructions aimed at the agent. Buffers up to 20 advisories per session and drains them on the next transform, the model call that reads the fetch result. Never writes stdout/stderr. |\n45: | `cli-dispatch-audit.js` | `dispatch`, `dispatch-guard` | `tool.execute.before` and `tool.execute.after` for Bash | `tool.execute.before` blocks a CLI dispatch that breaks a hard rule declared in the target skill's `SKILL.md` by throwing, and fails open on its own errors. `tool.execute.after` records redacted dispatch telemetry after completed CLI calls, recognizes an `opencode` or `opencode2` invocation and appends a bounded audit record. |\n46: | `codex-hooks-watchdog.js` | `codex-watchdog` | `event` (`session.created`) | Watches Codex hook installation and reports drift. Never affects the guarded session. |\n47: | `decision-capture.js` | `decision-capture` | `tool.execute.after` for `question`, `experimental.chat.system.transform` | Queues each answered `question` call for the next full-auto save under the same key as the save-time sweep, and pushes one system line when the count of answers still waiting for a decision record rises. Fails open and never changes the tool output. |\n48: | `env-secret-guard.js` | `env-secret-guard` | `tool.execute.before`, `tool` (`env_secret_list_keys`, `env_secret_validate`, `env_secret_fingerprint`) | Refuses a file tool or shell command that would read or write a dotenv file by throwing, through the shared core in `../hooks/env-secret-guard/`. Its three tools list keys, check syntax and fingerprint one value without returning any value. Fails open on its own errors. |\n49: | `mcp-route-guard.js` | `mcp-route-guard` | `tool.execute.before` | Advises when native MCP calls should use Code Mode. Persists to a bounded, rotated workspace log. |\n50: | `overlay-guard.js` | `overlay-guard` | `tool.execute.before` for write tools | Refuses a write tool that names a file a vendored tree's overlay owns, by throwing, through the shared core in `../hooks/overlay-guard/`. The reason names the overlay file to edit instead. A write it cannot judge is allowed, and its notes go to a bounded, rotated log because OpenCode paints stdout and stderr onto the prompt line. |\n51: | `opencode-goal.js` | `goal` | `event` (`session.created`/`status`/`idle`/`deleted`, `message.updated`/`message.part.updated`, `permission.asked`/`replied`, `question.asked`/`replied`/`rejected`), `experimental.chat.system.transform`, `tool` (goal tools) | Holds a text goal or binds a session to a packet `goal.md` and injects the objective slice built from it (via `../hooks/goal/lib/goal-slice.cjs`). Largest plugin in this folder. Owns the goal state machine, supervisor, continuation, capabilities and the goal tool surface including `bind`, `resent` and `packet`. |\n52: | `session-cleanup.js` | `session-cleanup` | `event` (`session.created`/`deleted`), `experimental.chat.system.transform`, `dispose` | Performs bounded session and host cleanup. Startup guards and teardown cleanup. Reaps MCP helper processes via `session-cleanup.sh`. |\n53: | `session-history-lookup.js` | `session-lifecycle` | `event` (`session.compacted`), `experimental.chat.system.transform` | On `session.compacted`, saves a history locator keyed by the checkout root above the session folder, then pushes the shared `## History Lookup` section into each request's system array while the locator is under 30 minutes old. Fails open: no build, no checkout or a failed save means no section. |\n54: | `session-relaunch.js` | `session-relaunch` | `event` (`message.updated`/`message.part.updated`/`session.idle`), `experimental.chat.system.transform` | Tracks usage and seed text. Inside `skilled-loop`, while this session has no matching relaunch record, it pushes the active nudge on every request. Outside the loop the nudge asks for a note and `keep` instead of `prepare`, because only the wrapper can relaunch the session. While a matching seed's note is open, it injects the seed on each request, then a claimed reminder once the note is claimed. It arms the record on `session.idle`. The plugin runs the detached killer with `node` because OpenCode hosts plugins on Bun. |\n55: | `sk-code-post-edit-quality.js` | `post-edit-quality` | `tool.execute.before`, `tool.execute.after`, `experimental.chat.system.transform` | Runs bounded post-edit quality checks. `tool.execute.before` stashes the edited path keyed by callID, because the after hook has no file path. `tool.execute.after` runs the router core. Findings drain on the next transform and are recorded to a workspace log. |\n56: | `sk-git-message-gate.js` | `git-message-gate` (no kill switch) | `tool.execute.before` for `bash` | Refuses a `git commit` message, `gh pr` description or new branch name that breaks the repository's own sk-git templates by throwing. Allows what the shared gate cannot parse, but refuses when the repository's rules exist and cannot be read. It has no kill switch, because the commit and push rules carry no bypass on any surface. |\n57: | `sk-git-preflight-advisory.js` | `git-preflight` | `tool.execute.before` for `bash`, `experimental.chat.system.transform` | Advises on Git command scope before execution. Buffers at most 20 advisory events and drains them on the next transform. Never prints. |\n58: | `system-completion-sentinel.js` | `completion` | `event` (`session.created`/`idle`) | Checks completion evidence at session lifecycle points. On `session.idle`, resolves completion state via `ctx.client.session.messages()` (guarded, fail-open). Throttled-sweeps the shared state dir on `session.created`. |\n59: | `system-deep-loop-guard.js` | `task-dispatch` | `event` (`session.created`), `tool.execute.before` | Checks deep-loop Task dispatches and repeat handoffs. Sweeps stale loop-guard state on `session.created`. |\n60: | `system-dist-freshness-guard.js` | `dist-freshness` | `tool.execute.before`, `event` (`session.created`/`deleted`), `experimental.chat.system.transform` | Reports stale compiled outputs and invalidates diagnostics. Short-circuits the whole plugin at factory entry when disabled. |\n61: | `system-skill-advisor.js` | `skill-advisor` | `experimental.chat.system.transform`, `event`, `tool` (`spec_kit_skill_advisor_status`) | Injects skill-routing guidance per prompt and exposes advisor status. Spawns the advisor subprocess only on a cache miss, behind a TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup. |\n62: | `system-spec-gate.js` | `spec-gate` | `experimental.chat.system.transform`, `tool.execute.before`, `event` | Classifies and evaluates mutation-gate state. Classify best-effort fetches the last user message via `ctx.client`. Enforce throws `system-spec-gate:` on deny (OpenCode's deny signal). |\n63: | `system-speckit-completion.js` | `completion` | `tool` (read-only completion evidence) | Exposes read-only completion evidence. Checks the `completion` concern at factory entry. |\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: ## 3. SHARED LIB MODULES\n68: \n69: `lib/` holds the modules the entry files import. None of them is a plugin source, and the loader never registers one on its own.\n70: \n71: | File | Responsibility |\n72: |---|---|\n73: | `lib/v2-bridge.js` | Builds the dual export. `dualPlugin(id, server)` returns `{ id, setup, server }`: `setup` maps its hooks (event stream, `tool.execute.before`/`after`, `experimental.chat.system.transform`, tool definitions) onto OpenCode 2 registrations and returns a cleanup function, while `server` stays the unchanged OpenCode 1 factory. It reports v2 `shell` and `subagent` calls under their v1 names, announces each session once with `session.created`, and fills the message events v1 plugins read. Hook keys the bridge does not map are reported through `cleanup.skippedKeys`. |\n74: | `lib/tool-identity.js` | Identity helper for tool definitions. `tool(definition)` returns the definition unchanged, and `tool.schema` is zod, so one definition serves the v1 `tool` hook and the bridge's v2 tool registration. |\n75: | `lib/opencode-message-identity.js` | Stable transform identity and dedup state. `system-skill-advisor.js` uses it when `deduplicateTransforms` is on. |\n76: | `lib/skill-advisor-render.js` | Advisor fallback and compiled-route renderers, shared by the advisor plugin and its tests. |\n77: | `lib/classifier-injection-screen-core.js` | Constants and the session-id resolver for the injection-screen plugin. |\n78: | `lib/completion-sentinel-core.js` | Re-export of the completion-evidence sentinel core under `skills/system-spec-kit`. |\n79: | `lib/opencode-goal-core.js` | WeakMap registry that binds a goal plugin module's helpers to its server, so both entries read the same state. |\n80: | `lib/sk-code-post-edit-quality-core.js` | Constants, the callID-to-filePath correlation map and the finding formatter for the post-edit-quality plugin. |\n81: \n82: The bridge mapping is exercised against stub contexts in the test suites. On 2026-10-19 the doctor battery confirmed that all 17 plugins load on 1.19.3 and show active on 2.0.32. On 2026-10-20 a probe plugin on a private OpenCode 2.0.32 host received `session.created` and `session.deleted` under those names for sessions created and removed through the server API, and received `location.shutdown` instead of `server.instance.disposed` or `global.disposed`. The probe did not start a session with `opencode run`, and v2 can deliver session traffic before `session.created`, so the bridge synthesizes a `session.created` the first time it sees traffic for a session and drops a later duplicate (`specs/cli-external-orchestration/opencode-platform/002-opencode-v1-v2-dual-compat/024-plugin-inventory-guards/scratch/v2-events/README.txt`). `tests/plugin-inventory.json` lists every event name a bridged plugin handles that the bridge neither maps nor aliases, grouped as observed, not delivered or unverified. The `plugin-utilization` playbook in `cli-opencode` checks each plugin's effect in a live session; on 2026-10-20 all 17 acted on OpenCode 1.19.3 and 2.0.32.\n83: \n84: ---\n85: \n86: ## 4. TEST SUITES\n87: \n88: `tests/` holds the regression suites for the entry files. They run on the Node built-in test runner with hermetic stubs. The v2 suites are:\n89: \n90: | File | Covers |\n91: |---|---|\n92: | `tests/v2-bridge.test.cjs` | The bridge itself: event aliases and mappings, the v1 client adapters, hook registration and cleanup, tool result conversion and the `tool` identity contract. |\n93: | `tests/v2-hard-plugins.test.cjs` | Selected hard cases, not the plugin list: expected v2 registrations for `system-completion-sentinel`, `system-spec-gate`, `system-skill-advisor` and `opencode-goal` through a stub v2 context, plus behavior cases for the first four. It also requires a case for every native-dual exception in the inventory. |\n94: | `tests/v2-registration.test.cjs` | The dual export shape, at least one v2 registration and the cleanup function for every bridged plugin the inventory finds. |\n95: | `tests/plugin-inventory.test.cjs` | The inventory itself: every `.js` file is bridged or a listed native-dual exception, every bridged plugin maps all its hooks, and every event name a bridged plugin compares against is mapped, aliased or listed in `tests/plugin-inventory.json`. The event names come from parsing each plugin's `type` comparisons, `switch` statements on the event type and literal collections; any other kind of event check, such as `eventType.endsWith('.disposed')`, must be listed under `unscannedEventChecks` with a reason. |\n96: \n97: The plugin list comes from the folder, not from a hand-written list. `tests/helpers/plugin-inventory.cjs` treats a file as bridged when its source contains `dualPlugin('<file stem>'`, and `tests/v2-registration.test.cjs` then checks that its default export has the dual shape, and `tests/plugin-inventory.json` names the exceptions, with an empty `nativeDual` list. A new plugin file that is neither bridged nor listed fails `tests/plugin-inventory.test.cjs`.\n98: \n99: The remaining `*.test.cjs` files cover v1 behavior and adjacent contracts. `tests/README.md` indexes every suite, and `tests/helpers/` holds the shared fixtures.\n100: \n101: ---\n102: \n103: ## 5. DIRECTORY TREE\n104: \n105: ```text\n106: plugins/\n107: +-- README.md                              # this index\n108: +-- ARCHITECTURE.md                        # dual-version design, bridge and limits\n109: +-- ask-surface-guard.js\n110: +-- classifier-injection-screen.js\n111: +-- cli-dispatch-audit.js\n112: +-- codex-hooks-watchdog.js\n113: +-- decision-capture.js\n114: +-- env-secret-guard.js\n115: +-- mcp-route-guard.js\n116: +-- overlay-guard.js\n117: +-- opencode-goal.js\n118: +-- session-cleanup.js\n119: +-- session-history-lookup.js\n120: +-- session-relaunch.js\n121: +-- sk-code-post-edit-quality.js\n122: +-- sk-git-message-gate.js\n123: +-- sk-git-preflight-advisory.js\n124: +-- system-completion-sentinel.js\n125: +-- system-deep-loop-guard.js\n126: +-- system-dist-freshness-guard.js\n127: +-- system-skill-advisor.js\n128: +-- system-spec-gate.js\n129: +-- system-speckit-completion.js\n130: +-- lib/\n131: |   +-- classifier-injection-screen-core.js\n132: |   +-- completion-sentinel-core.js\n133: |   +-- opencode-goal-core.js\n134: |   +-- opencode-message-identity.js\n135: |   +-- sk-code-post-edit-quality-core.js\n136: |   +-- skill-advisor-render.js\n137: |   +-- tool-identity.js\n138: |   `-- v2-bridge.js\n139: `-- tests/\n140:     +-- README.md\n141:     +-- helpers/\n142:     +-- plugin-inventory.test.cjs\n143:     +-- plugin-inventory.json\n144:     +-- v2-bridge.test.cjs\n145:     +-- v2-hard-plugins.test.cjs\n146:     +-- v2-registration.test.cjs\n147:     `-- *.test.cjs                         # the remaining plugin suites\n148: ```\n149: \n150: ---\n151: \n152: ## 6. KEY FILES\n153: \n154: | File | Responsibility |\n155: |---|---|\n156: | `opencode-goal.js` | The largest plugin. Owns the goal state machine, supervisor, continuation, capabilities and the goal tool surface. Hooks session, message, permission and question events, `experimental.chat.system.transform` and a family of goal tools. |\n157: | `system-skill-advisor.js` | Spawns the skill-advisor subprocess on a prompt-cache miss, manages a TTL+LRU prompt cache with in-flight dedup, integrates transform-dedup where the opt-in same-message dedup bases its decision on the full advisor block before directive-lifecycle reduction and suppresses a repeated transform for the same message and exposes `spec_kit_skill_advisor_status`. |\n158: | `system-spec-gate.js` | Maps OpenCode's transport onto the runtime-neutral spec-gate core. Classify best-effort fetches the last user message via `ctx.client`. Enforce throws `system-spec-gate:` on deny. `event` sweeps, advances and evicts state. |\n159: | `sk-code-post-edit-quality.js` | Correlates `tool.execute.before` file paths to `tool.execute.after` callIDs, runs the post-edit router core and drains findings on the next transform. |\n160: | `lib/v2-bridge.js` | Builds the dual export for every bridged plugin. `server` is the unchanged v1 factory, and `setup` maps its hooks onto OpenCode 2 registrations and returns a cleanup function. |\n161: | `lib/tool-identity.js` | The `tool` identity helper. Returns a v1 tool definition unchanged and carries zod as `tool.schema`. |\n162: | `lib/opencode-message-identity.js` | Shared stable transform identity and dedup state used by the advisor plugin. |\n163: \n164: ---\n165: \n166: ## 7. CONFIGURATION\n167: \n168: Every plugin except `session-relaunch.js` is enabled by default. Truthy disable values are `1`, `true`, `yes` and `on` (case-insensitive) for the shared resolver. Each plugin that honors a switch checks `isHookEnabled('<concern>')` at entry (some at factory entry, some per-hook) and stops its main work when disabled. Housekeeping can still run, such as the deep-loop guard's stale-state sweep on `session.created`. The one exception named in the overview does not.\n169: \n170: | Variable | Effect |\n171: |---|---|\n172: | `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED=1` | Master switch that disables every plugin in this folder that reads `hook-flags.cjs`, along with every other repo hook. It does not reach `sk-git-message-gate.js`. |\n173: | Per-concern switch | The default name is `SYSTEM_<CONCERN>_DISABLED` (e.g. `SYSTEM_SPEC_GATE_DISABLED`, `SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLED`). Six concerns use another canonical name: `OPENCODE_GOAL_DISABLED`, `CLI_DISPATCH_AUDIT_DISABLED`, `MCP_ROUTE_GUARD_DISABLED`, `CODEX_HOOKS_WATCHDOG_DISABLED`, `SK_GIT_PREFLIGHT_DISABLED` and `SK_CODE_POST_EDIT_QUALITY_DISABLED`. |\n174: | Older aliases | `hook-flags.cjs` still honors older names, e.g. `SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_HOOK_DISABLED` and `OPENCODE_GOAL_PLUGIN_DISABLED`. See each plugin's own README. |\n175: | `SYSTEM_SESSION_RELAUNCH_OPENCODE_ENABLED`, `SYSTEM_SESSION_RELAUNCH_ENABLED` | Turn on `session-relaunch.js`, which ships off. A non-empty OpenCode flag decides for OpenCode (`1` on, `0` off), and the global flag decides otherwise. `SYSTEM_SESSION_RELAUNCH_DISABLED=1` and the master switch still win. Also read from `.skilled/hooks/hook-flags.env`. |\n176: \n177: Optional tuning: `SPECKIT_OPENCODE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` (default 3000, owned by `system-skill-advisor`), `SYSTEM_OPENCODE_TRANSFORM_DEDUP=1` (opt-in transform dedup for the advisor plugin), plus per-plugin cache/budget/timeout envs documented in each plugin's README.\n178: \n179: Set a flag inline for one command, export it for a session or persist it in `.skilled/hooks/hook-flags.env` (copied from `hook-flags.env.example`, gitignored). The environment always wins over the file, so a persisted default can be overridden for a single session. Only switches read through `hook-flags.cjs` can be persisted there.\n180: \n181: ---\n182: \n183: ## 8. BOUNDARIES AND FLOW\n184: \n185: | Boundary | Rule |\n186: |---|---|\n187: | Transport only | Plugin files own the OpenCode transport boundary. Shared policy and runtime-neutral logic belongs under the owning skill. |\n188: | Dual export | Every plugin file default-exports one plain object `{ id, setup, server }`: `setup` is the OpenCode 2 (recommended) entry and `server` the OpenCode 1 (fallback) entry. `lib/` modules are shared helpers, never plugin sources. |\n189: | No stdout/stderr | Plugins do not print to stdout or stderr in normal operation. The only writes are opt-in debug output, such as the goal plugin's debug env. OpenCode overlays those onto the TUI prompt line and corrupts the interactive session. Findings go through `experimental.chat.system.transform`, tool returns or bounded workspace logs. |\n190: | Fail-open | All advisory checks fail open. A missing payload, a subprocess timeout, a parse failure or any internal error resolves to a no-op, or for the read-only completion tool to a payload that marks the missing sections unavailable. The skill advisor is the exception to the no-op because on a subprocess timeout, a parse failure, a missing prompt or an internal error it injects its status-headed fallback (an `Advisor: outage (...)` or `Advisor: prompt skipped.` line above the directives block) and never blocks the prompt. The git message gate and the dispatch guard reject by default. Spec-gate rejection is opt-in through `SYSTEM_SPEC_GATE_ENFORCE`, and deep-loop guard rejection through `SYSTEM_DEEP_LOOP_GUARD_REJECT=1` or `SYSTEM_DEEP_LOOP_GUARD_REJECT_LOOP=1`. |\n191: | Kill-switch | Every plugin except the one named in the overview honors its per-concern kill-switch via the shared `hook-flags.cjs` resolver plus the master `SYSTEM_HOOKS_DISABLED`. A disabled plugin stops its main work, and the goal and advisor status tools stay registered to report that. Housekeeping such as the deep-loop guard's stale-state sweep can still run. |\n192: | Bounded | Subprocess spawns, pending-event buffers and workspace logs are bounded, as is the skill advisor's TTL+LRU prompt cache (per-plugin limits documented in each plugin's README). The advisor's workspace-surface cache instead keeps one entry per workspace directory the process has seen, so it is not capped. |\n193: | Tests | Plugin tests stay under `tests/`. |\n194: \n195: ---\n196: \n197: ## 9. HOOK MODEL\n198: \n199: Plugin factories register some subset of:\n200: \n201: - `tool`: tools exposed to the model (e.g. `spec_kit_skill_advisor_status`, the goal tools, the read-only completion-evidence tool).\n202: - `tool.execute.before`: pre-tool evaluation (spec-gate enforce, dispatch guard, env-secret-guard, git message gate, git-preflight, mcp-route-guard, post-edit-quality path stash, deep-loop guard).\n203: - `tool.execute.after`: post-tool evaluation (cli-dispatch-audit, post-edit-quality run, injection screen).\n204: - `experimental.chat.system.transform`: per-turn system-context injection (advisor, spec-gate classify, goal, session-cleanup, session-relaunch nudge and seed, git-preflight drain, post-edit-quality drain, dist-freshness and injection-screen drain).\n205: - `event`: lifecycle and activity handlers (`session.created`/`status`/`idle`/`deleted`/`resumed`/`compacted`/`compact`, `message.updated`/`message.part.updated`, the goal plugin's permission and question events, `server.instance.disposed`/`global.disposed`).\n206: - `dispose`: teardown when the host unloads the plugin (session-cleanup).\n207: \n208: The bridge in `lib/v2-bridge.js` maps that set onto OpenCode 2 registrations: `event` to the event stream, `tool.execute.before`/`after` to the tool execute hooks, `experimental.chat.system.transform` to the session context hook and `tool` definitions to the tool transform. A key outside the set is reported through `cleanup.skippedKeys`.\n209: \n210: ---\n211: \n212: ## 10. VALIDATION\n213: \n214: Run the Node regression command from the repository root:\n215: \n216: ```bash\n217: node --test .opencode/plugins/tests/*.test.cjs\n218: ```\n219: \n220: Expected result: Node discovers every current CJS test file and reports the suite result. A failing test is a validation failure.\n221: \n222: ```bash\n223: (\n224:   rc=0\n225:   for f in .opencode/plugins/*.js\n226:   do node --check \"$f\" || rc=1\n227:   done\n228:   exit \"$rc\"\n229: )\n230: ```\n231: \n232: Expected result: exit status 0, with no syntax errors across every plugin. Any failing file makes the command exit 1. `node --check` checks syntax only and does not resolve imports.\n233: \n234: Check both OpenCode versions with the doctor battery:\n235: \n236: ```bash\n237: node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --family=v1\n238: node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/opencode-version-battery.cjs --family=v2\n239: ```\n240: \n241: Expected result: each exits 0, reporting all plugin files active on OpenCode 2 (recommended) and all discovered on OpenCode 1 (fallback). The v2 run starts its own private server.\n242: \n243: ---\n244: \n245: ## 11. RELATED\n246: \n247: - [`ARCHITECTURE.md`](./ARCHITECTURE.md): the dual-version design, the bridge, the kill switches and the known limits.\n248: - [`tests/README.md`](./tests/README.md): the plugin regression suites.\n249: - [`tests/helpers/README.md`](./tests/helpers/README.md): the shared test helpers.\n250: - [`../hooks/README.md`](../hooks/README.md): the unified hooks tree with the kill-switch index and coverage matrix. Plugins are mirrored there as the OpenCode adapter for their concern (`spec-gate`, `skill-advisor`, `git-preflight`, `git-message-gate`, `session-cleanup`, `injection-screen`).\n251: - [`../skills/`](../skills/): the shared skill cores these plugins adapt.\n252: \n253: <!-- readme-check\n254: allow-count four plugins | the sentence counts the plugins that reject by default by behavior, not the plugin files in this folder\n255: allow-count 17 plugin files | the sentence reports the 2026-10-19 battery run and names the plugins added since\n256: allow-count 17 plugins | the sentence reports the 2026-10-19 battery run, not the current folder\n257: -->"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/plugins/README.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546524711,"end":1794546525302}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546525305,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb172a1ffej1jOZ3ysNoYkNr","part":{"partID":"prt_1d34ee355001U4kUrMu23gvk6p","sessionID":"ses_e2cb172a1ffej1jOZ3ysNoYkNr","messageID":"msg_1d34ebf1b001qZDR6RYqecXzRd","type":"tool","id":"call_99f13042587a423484130dc0","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCES AND WORDS\n160: \n161: **One idea per sentence.** Short and declarative. When a sentence stacks clauses, split it. Vary the rhythm, and prefer prose when a list would fragment a single argument.\n162: \n163: **State the relation between sentences.** A sentence says how it attaches to the one before: because, but, therefore, for example. When it explains how something works, it names the moving parts: what acts, what it acts on, what changes.\n164: \n165: **Plain words by default.** Reserve exact names for the things that have them: languages, frameworks, APIs, dependencies, commands. Introduce unavoidable jargon one term at a time, in a sentence that defines it by use.\n166: \n167: **No terse machine register.** Replace arrows and symbols standing in for verbs with action words. For stacked noun phrases, say who does what, using the [HVR nominalization guidance](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md#nominalization-and-stacked-compression). Explain each ID, abbreviation or status code in plain words, and say what a bare count or label measures. Turn fragments into complete sentences.\n168: \n169: **Name an identifier only where the reader acts on it.** A path, hash, flag or ID earns its place when the reader will run, open or check it. Elsewhere, say what the thing is. Keep to one or two numbers a sentence, and when the numbers are the point, give each its own sentence or bullet.\n170: \n171: The failure this prevents: a technically correct reply the reader has to decode, where\n172: they supply a link the writer never stated and cannot find the few names and figures they\n173: need.\n174: \n175: ---\n176: \n177: ## 7. PUNCTUATION THE READER TRIPS ON\n178: \n179: - **Never use an em dash.** Use a comma, a full stop or a colon, whichever the sentence wanted.\n180: - **No semicolon.** Two sentences, or a conjunction.\n181: - **No serial comma.** Drop it before the `and` or `or` that closes a list.\n182: \n183: The failure this prevents: dashes read as authored voice to a human and as a tell to a\n184: reader who has seen a lot of generated text.\n185: \n186: The full standard is the Human Voice Rules, which `sk-doc` routes to. Load all of it when writing a document. A reply takes its voice half, the directives, vocabulary lists and tell lists, and leaves its document-structure half.\n187: \n188: ---\n189: \n190: ## 8. LONG STRETCHES AND WHAT THEY FOUND\n191: \n192: **Say where you are going before a long stretch.** Before work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short numbered list of what you will do and what to expect at each checkpoint. Update it when it changes. Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you are going.\n193: \n194: **Attach a concrete time estimate when the stretch is long.** Minutes or hours, based on a named earlier run, or marked as an assumption. An estimate informs and never gates. Outside that case, do not open a task with one nobody asked for, and never use one to argue against starting. When the task is too big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering, start there and say what is deferred.\n195: \n196: **A synthesis is not a file path.** When a long run produces findings, the message carries the answer, the few findings behind it, the uncertainty that matters and what was ruled out. The artifact path follows them. Per-iteration narration and internal metrics stay out. Where a run has its own completion contract, as the deep-loop modes do, that contract wins.\n197: \n198: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n199: reverse-engineer a plan from, or a path and a count handed over in place of a report.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 9. WHEN THE READER DID NOT FOLLOW\n204: \n205: \"I don't follow\", \"what?\" or \"too abstract\" calls for a change of modality: a concrete\n206: example, a numbered sequence, a smaller first step or a picture using the runtime's visual\n207: capability. Do not repeat the same explanation at greater length.\n208: \n209: \"say that more plainly\", \"in simple terms\" or \"rewrite that\" calls for a plain\n210: re-render: a copy edit that rewords without reordering, cutting or adding. Every claim,\n211: number, caveat and conclusion keeps its strength. Code, commands, paths, identifiers,\n212: quotations and numbers stay byte-exact. The wording standard is\n213: [hvr-rules.md](../skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/references/hvr-rules.md). If a\n214: plainer word would change what a sentence claims, keep the original.\n215: \n216: The failure this prevents: the same explanation repeats, or the plain rewrite changes the claim.\n217: \n218: ---\n219: \n220: ## 10. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n221: \n222: - **Not a constraint on rigor.** Nothing here softens a claim, a caveat, or a verification standard owned by [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) or [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n223: - **Not a voice to perform.** When honoring a rule here would weaken the answer, keep the answer.\n224: - **Not a license to omit.** \"Match length to the question\" is about the reader's need,\n225:   never about leaving out what they have to know.\n226: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n227:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n228: \n229: ---\n230: \n231: ## 11. SELF-CHECK\n232: \n233: - [ ] The first line carries the answer or the action, not a label, an announcement or a setup, and the outcome is complete by the second line.\n234: - [ ] I reached the verdict by analysis, recommended one path with its trade-off and stated the assumptions I made.\n235: - [ ] Where the request was ambiguous or I delivered a different version of it, the first lines say which reading I took and why.\n236: - [ ] Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward, and multi-step work reads as a numbered list.\n237: - [ ] No group runs past five items, nothing was dropped to keep it under, and a tangent appears once at the end or not at all.\n238: - [ ] Every sentence carries information, each fact appears once, and no runtime instruction to plan privately was copied into the reply.\n239: - [ ] Length matches what the reader asked, no table stands in a reply, and nothing was compressed past the point where the reader has to re-expand it.\n240: - [ ] Every sentence carries one idea in plain words and says how it attaches to the one before.\n241: - [ ] Arrows, bare IDs, counts and fragments were turned into sentences, and every path, hash or flag is one the reader will act on.\n242: - [ ] No em dash, no semicolon, no serial comma.\n243: - [ ] Before a long stretch I said where I was going, with a concrete time estimate when it was long, and no estimate argued against starting.\n244: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n245: - [ ] When the reader did not follow, I changed modality, and a plain re-render kept every claim, number and caveat at the same strength.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md, lines 1-245\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication\"\n3: description: \"Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"cut filler\"\n6:   - \"empty opener\"\n7:   - \"match length to the question\"\n8:   - \"no tables\"\n9:   - \"can you say that more plainly\"\n10:   - \"I don't follow\"\n11:   - \"too abstract\"\n12:   - \"number the steps\"\n13:   - \"item cap\"\n14:   - \"reply too long\"\n15:   - \"say each fact once\"\n16:   - \"one idea per sentence\"\n17:   - \"plain words\"\n18:   - \"em dash\"\n19:   - \"reads like terminal output\"\n20:   - \"concise is not compressed\"\n21:   - \"recommend one option\"\n22:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n23:   - \"say where you are going\"\n24:   - \"time estimate\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.5.0.0\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Communication\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before writing any substantive reply.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out, a status.\n38: - Drafting or editing a sentence a reader will read.\n39: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n40: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n41: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n42: - The reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version.\n43: \n44: ## The rule\n45: \n46: **Write so the reader can act after one pass: the answer first, and nothing in the reply\n47: that does not carry information.**\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 1. THE REGISTER YOU ARE IN\n52: \n53: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n54: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n55: \n56: **At a boundary: dense.** You are at a boundary whenever the reader is about to decide\n57: something, act on something or take the work over: a handoff, a close-out, a decision\n58: point. Verdict first, then the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n59: Everything else is working.\n60: \n61: **Complex topic, simple words.** When the subject is technical or layered, explain it plainly the first time, not only after the reader asks: what it is, why it matters to them, what they do next. Use a term only when the reader needs it, and define it on first use. Simpler words, same claim: never drop a caveat or a number to get there.\n62: \n63: The failure this prevents: the wrong register. Narration nobody reads, the verdict buried\n64: in working notes or the working notes inflated into a report.\n65: \n66: ---\n67: \n68: ## 2. LENGTH\n69: \n70: **Match length to the question.** Length is earned by the reader's need, never by the work you did to get there.\n71: \n72: **Choose by reader impact before you write.** Decide who will read this and what they already hold. Keep what the reader would notice, act on or\n73: decide differently because of. Merge the items that have the same effect for them. How the\n74: work was done stays out unless it changes how far the reader can trust the result.\n75: \n76: **Concise is not compressed.** Concise cuts what the reader does not need and stops. Compressed cuts further and takes the connective tissue with it: the because, the second half of a contrast, the noun that a later \"this\" points back to. After a cut, check the joints. Every cut this file asks for answers to that floor.\n77: \n78: The failure this prevents: the answer is in there, and they did not find it, under a\n79: record of the work they never needed. Or the cut went too far, and the reader rebuilds the\n80: argument the reply no longer states.\n81: \n82: **No tables in a reply,** except the in-flight block in\n83: [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §6. Use a sentence for one or two\n84: facts and bullets for parallel items.\n85: \n86: The failure this prevents: the reader parses a grid to learn what one sentence would\n87: have said.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. CUT FILLER\n92: \n93: Every sentence carries information. The recurring offenders:\n94: \n95: - **Empty openers:** \"Great question\", \"Let me take a look\", \"I'll now\".\n96: - **Restated summaries:** repeating back what you just said, one abstraction level up.\n97: - **Vague warnings:** \"be careful with this\", \"this can be tricky\", naming no failure. A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here.\n98: - **Corporate and marketing register:** \"robust\", \"seamless\", \"leverage\", \"best-in-class\".\n99: - **Narrating the obvious:** announcing a tool call the reader can see the result of.\n100: - **Leaked scaffolding:** a runtime line that tells you to plan privately, list what you\n101:   need next or batch your calls is answered in reasoning, never in the reply.\n102: \n103: **Say each fact once.** Each part of the reply has one job: the first lines carry the\n104: outcome, the body carries the detail, and the end carries the receipts and the handback. A\n105: sentence that repeats an earlier one goes, or it says something the reader does not know\n106: yet.\n107: \n108: The failure this prevents: filler trains the reader to skim, and then they skim the\n109: sentence that mattered.\n110: \n111: ---\n112: \n113: ## 4. THE ANSWER FIRST\n114: \n115: **The first line carries the payload.** The answer, the verdict or the action, in the\n116: first sentence, not a label for it. Test: read the first line on its own. If it told you the outcome, it did its job. Four habits break it: announcing (\"I will now\n117: check the tests\"), labelling (\"Overview:\"), the fragment opener (\"Context.\") and the set-up\n118: that promises the good part in a moment.\n119: \n120: **The rest of the outcome follows at once.** What the work concluded, what changed and what\n121: to do next are in the reader's hands by the second line. The receipts come after that, and\n122: the handback comes last, per [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n123: End when the answer is done. The closing-deletion test: a last line that only asks whether\n124: anything else is needed, or recaps what the reply just said, deletes.\n125: \n126: **Reach the verdict by analysis, then put it first.** The order of the reply is not the order of the thinking. If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about the state of the evidence.\n127: \n128: **Recommend one path.** Name its main trade-off, and mention an alternative only when it could change the decision. Mark must-do work apart from nice-to-have. State an assumption you made where evidence was missing, because the reader can correct a visible assumption and cannot correct a silent one.\n129: \n130: **An ambiguous request still gets the answer first.** When two readings lead to different answers, answer the likelier one and say in one line which reading you took. When they lead to materially different work, ask before starting it, per [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md) §1. When you deliver a different version of the ask, the first lines say what changed and why.\n131: \n132: The failure this prevents: a payload that waits behind an announcement or a label, a survey\n133: of options where one recommendation was owed, a swapped or misread request nobody catches,\n134: or a last line that is a farewell and adds nothing.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. HOW THE REPLY MOVES\n139: \n140: **Each paragraph stands alone and carries the reader forward.** A reader who lands mid-reply can act on the paragraph in front of them, and each one picks up where the last one landed: what changed, what it implies, what comes next.\n141: \n142: **Number the steps when there is more than one.** Anything the reader must run, check or\n143: answer gets its own number, one step per line, the number at the start of the line. Numbers\n144: bracketed inside a sentence, (1) then (2) then (3), are a paragraph wearing numbers.\n145: \n146: **No group shows more than five items.** Split a longer set into labelled groups of five or\n147: fewer, or show the five that matter most and say in one line how many are held back, \"the\n148: first five of nine\". The rest is retained, not discarded, and appears when the reader asks.\n149: \n150: **Suppress the tangent.** The reply answers the question it was asked. A second issue worth\n151: raising gets offered once, at the end, in one line. The reader decides whether it happens.\n152: \n153: The failure this prevents: the reader assembles the order themselves, loses track of which\n154: step they have done, mistakes a shortened list for the complete one, or waits for the\n155: answer behind a second issue.\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 6. SENTENCE