{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794546527938,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb172a1ffej1jOZ3ysNoYkNr","part":{"id":"prt_1d34eeec2001ueiH31hXzZYSE0","sessionID":"ses_e2cb172a1ffej1jOZ3ysNoYkNr","messageID":"msg_1d34ee851001B8To8F2tovZ7ml","type":"step-start","snapshot":"b0a2cb9e4c645744589c4ef9381a9209575a76bb"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546530128,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb172a1ffej1jOZ3ysNoYkNr","part":{"partID":"prt_1d34ef518001EcAl0dPchXxeZP","sessionID":"ses_e2cb172a1ffej1jOZ3ysNoYkNr","messageID":"msg_1d34ee851001B8To8F2tovZ7ml","type":"tool","id":"call_25052d2278284a65a600235a","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546529560,"end":1794546530128}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546530132,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb172a1ffej1jOZ3ysNoYkNr","part":{"partID":"prt_1d34ef6380017v6cFX6Aq1fqVo","sessionID":"ses_e2cb172a1ffej1jOZ3ysNoYkNr","messageID":"msg_1d34ee851001B8To8F2tovZ7ml","type":"tool","id":"call_3b32d58558f248b3a1507b91","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md, lines 1-153\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-doc\n3: description: \"Documentation & component authoring hub: skills, agents, commands, READMEs, catalogs, playbooks, changelogs, goals, frontmatter.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 2.9.13.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-util\n8: ---\n9: \n10: <!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n11: \n12: # Documentation Authoring Hub (sk-doc)\n13: \n14: One advisor identity, fifteen workflow modes across fourteen packets, one shared sk-create-quality-control backbone.\n15: `sk-doc` is the parent hub for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring.\n16: It holds NO per-packet logic: it routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each packet keeps its own contract in its nested folder.\n17: The cross-cutting sk-create-quality-control pipeline (validators, global standards, frontmatter/llms/template assets) lives once in `shared/` and is consumed by every packet.\n18: \n19: **Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`; `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value, so every hub-root JSON routing file states the same release; `ROUTER.md` keeps its own document version.\n20: \n21: ---\n22: \n23: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n24: \n25: Use this skill for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring, and for document-quality work. The hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested packet.\n26: \n27: | Mode | Use it for | Packet | Command |\n28: |------|------------|--------|---------|\n29: | **sk-create-skill** | Scaffold an OpenCode skill (and, via `sk-create-skill-parent`, a parent hub with nested mode packets) | `sk-create-skill/` | `/create:skill`, `/create:skill-parent` |\n30: | **sk-create-readme** | Author a folder README, general or code-folder | `sk-create-readme/` | `/create:readme` |\n31: | **sk-create-agent** | Scaffold an OpenCode agent (permission/authority frontmatter) | `sk-create-agent/` | `/create:agent` |\n32: | **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n33: | **sk-create-feature-catalog** | Author a feature-catalog inventory package | `sk-create-feature-catalog/` | `/create:feature-catalog` |\n34: | **sk-create-manual-testing-playbook** | Author a manual-testing-playbook package | `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/` | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` |\n35: | **sk-create-benchmark** | Author MCP-promotion and behavior packages or inputs | `sk-create-benchmark/` | `/create:benchmark` |\n36: | **sk-create-changelog** | Author a global component changelog entry (version bump + topology-aware placement). Packet-local changelogs are deprecated | `sk-create-changelog/` | `/create:changelog` |\n37: | **sk-create-diff** | Produce a local, Git-free before/after review of an edited document (text/Markdown/HTML/DOCX/text-PDF) as a self-contained HTML report | `sk-create-diff/` | `/create:diff` |\n38: | **sk-create-repo-rule** | Create, revise or retire a repo-local rule under `repo-rules/` and wire it into `REPO RULES.md` (four decision tests refuse most requests) | `sk-create-repo-rule/` | `/create:repo-rule` |\n39: | **sk-create-with-human-voice** | Apply the Human Voice Rules to prose, or score prose against them, with a scope gate and a re-scan | `sk-create-with-human-voice/` | `/create:with-human-voice` |\n40: | **sk-create-frontmatter** | Own the YAML frontmatter contract: per-class field rules, the description budget, and the 4-part `version` derivation | `sk-create-frontmatter/` | — (routes via aliases) |\n41: | **sk-create-goal** | Author or revise a packet `goal.md`: the durable directive, three to seven self-contained completion criteria, and the phase-parent binding table (session-goal setting stays with the goal hooks) | `sk-create-goal/` | `/create:goal` |\n42: | **sk-create-quality-control** | Validate / score / optimize an EXISTING document (extract → DQI → HVR → validate) | `sk-create-quality-control/` | (routes via aliases) |\n43: \n44: ### When NOT to Use\n45: - Code implementation, tests, or debugging — use `sk-code`.\n46: - Git worktree, branch, commit, PR, or release work — use `sk-git`.\n47: - Spec-folder workflow / memory / save context — use `system-spec-kit`.\n48: - A measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source) — use `sk-design-md-generator`.\n49: - The `shared/` backbone is consumed by the packets, not invoked as a user workflow.\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n54: \n55: Routing is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\n56: Stage 1 (hub → mode) selects a mode: `hub-router.json` `vocabularyClasses` is the one authored routing vocabulary.\n57: The `mode-registry.json` aliases, each packet's `Keyword triggers:` line and the root `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against it.\n58: `sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-hub-vocabulary-source.cjs` fails on the `alias`, `stage-two`, `triggers` and `packets` legs; its `stage-two-reverse` leg only reports.\n59: Stage 2 (mode → leaves) lives in the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md), which maps the request's authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources that mode loads.\n60: The two layers stay separate: the hub never emits leaf paths, and the surface router never re-decides the mode.\n61: The advisor routes every documentation and authoring query to the single identity `sk-doc`.\n62: Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"`.\n63: Act on the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\n64: Compiled routing is the default.\n65: An unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation, because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n66: Run the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n71: \n72: ### Terms\n73: \n74: - **`workflowMode`**: the public packet key a request resolves to, such as `sk-create-skill`.\n75: - **`packetKind`**: `workflow` for every sk-doc packet, because this hub has no surface axis.\n76: - **`backendKind`**: `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators and `sk-create-quality-control` for the quality-control mode.\n77: - **packet**: a nested `sk-create-*` folder that keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets and scripts.\n78: - **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n79: - **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource and bundle rules.\n80: - **`vocabularyClasses`**: the one authored routing vocabulary that the registry aliases, the `Keyword triggers:` lines and the `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against.\n81: - **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, which map an authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources its mode loads.\n82: - **leaf**: a canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n83: - **`defer`**: the outcome that asks for disambiguation, which this hub reaches because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n84: - **shared backbone**: the `shared/` copy of the sk-create-quality-control pipeline that every packet consumes.\n85: - **`graph-metadata.json`**: the one advisor identity for the whole skill, which no packet or `shared/` directory carries.\n86: \n87: ### Layout\n88: ```\n89: sk-doc/\n90:   SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-packet logic)\n91:   ROUTER.md              # stage-two surface router (authoring intent -> leaf sets)\n92:   mode-registry.json     # the fifteen-mode discriminator + advisorRouting (single source of truth)\n93:   hub-router.json        # router signals + vocabulary classes\n94:   description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n95:   graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n96:   changelog/  manual-testing-playbook/  benchmark/  feature-catalog/\n97:   sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n98:   sk-create-feature-catalog/  sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/\n99:   sk-create-benchmark/  sk-create-changelog/  sk-create-diff/\n100:   sk-create-repo-rule/  sk-create-frontmatter/  sk-create-goal/\n101:   sk-create-with-human-voice/  sk-create-quality-control/    # nested workflow packets\n102:   scripts/               # facade symlinks -> shared/ + owning packets (tool paths only)\n103:   shared/                # sk-create-quality-control backbone: validators, global standards, shared assets\n104: ```\n105: \n106: Each packet is self-contained (its own `SKILL.md`, `README.md`, `changelog/`, and moved `references/`/`assets/`/`scripts/`) and carries **no** `graph-metadata.json`, so the advisor discovers exactly one `sk-doc` identity.\n107: \n108: ### Shared backbone\n109: `shared/` holds the universal sk-create-quality-control pipeline consumed by every packet:\n110: - Generic validator scripts in `shared/scripts/`.\n111: - Cross-cutting standards and vocabulary in `shared/references/`.\n112: - Shared templates in `shared/assets/`.\n113: \n114: The generic validator `shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` counts and resolves the `<path>.<ext>:<line>` citations in every tracked skill doc.\n115: It makes zero model calls by default.\n116: `--jev` runs the backend and needs `--out <dir>`, so every call is recorded, and the Jev arm and the advisory live in the sibling `shared/scripts/classifier-cite-drift-scan.mjs`.\n117: `validate_document.py` runs the same check as a non-blocking advisory on the document it validates.\n118: It prints `cite-drift advisory:` lines only when a Jev credential is stored, never changes the exit code, and `JEV_FEATURE_CITE_DRIFT=0` (older name: `SKDOC_CITE_DRIFT_CHECK=0`) skips it.\n119: The `sk-doc/scripts/` root directory keeps facade symlinks pointing inward to `shared/` and the owning packets so tool paths resolve.\n120: There are no hub-root `assets/` or `references/` aggregation directories: consumers reference each packet's own `assets/`/`references/` or the `shared/` backbone directly.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 4. RULES\n125: \n126: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n127: - **ALWAYS** resolve a packet through `mode-registry.json`; never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n128: - **ALWAYS** keep authoring contracts in the packets; the hub stays routing-only.\n129: - **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one identity.\n130: - **ALWAYS** keep the root `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` in sync with `leaf-manifest.json` and `leaf-aliases.json` — the leaf sets dual-read to canonical typed pairs at the one contract boundary.\n131: - **ALWAYS** keep the sk-create-quality-control pipeline as one shared source under `shared/`, consumed by the packets.\n132: - **ALWAYS** keep changelogs as real files at the hub and in each packet — never symlinked.\n133: \n134: ### ⛔ NEVER\n135: - **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a packet or `shared/`.\n136: - **NEVER** put per-packet authoring logic in the hub.\n137: - **NEVER** add a surface axis or a `surfaceBundle` outcome — sk-doc is workflow-only.\n138: \n139: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n140: - A new documentation workflow is needed — extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet; do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n141: - A packet would become a near-empty shell (generic doctrine, no type-specific behavior) — fold it into `shared/` instead.\n142: \n143: ---\n144: \n145: ## 5. REFERENCES\n146: \n147: - Registry: `mode-registry.json` (fifteen modes across fourteen packets, `packetKind: workflow`).\n148: - Hub router: `hub-router.json` (signals + vocabulary classes).\n149: - Surface router: `ROUTER.md` (authoring intent to packet-local leaf sets).\n150: - Advisor descriptor: `description.json`; skill-graph identity: `graph-metadata.json`.\n151: - Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n152: - Shared backbone: `shared/scripts/`, `shared/references/`, `shared/assets/`.\n153: - Parent-skill pattern: `sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md, lines 1-153\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-doc\n3: description: \"Documentation & component authoring hub: skills, agents, commands, READMEs, catalogs, playbooks, changelogs, goals, frontmatter.\"\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 2.9.13.0\n6: metadata:\n7:   family: sk-util\n8: ---\n9: \n10: <!-- Keywords: sk-doc, documentation, markdown, authoring, parent-hub, mode-registry, hub-router, workflowmode, packetkind, sk-create-skill, sk-create-readme, sk-create-agent, sk-create-command, sk-create-feature-catalog, sk-create-manual-testing-playbook, sk-create-benchmark, sk-create-changelog, sk-create-diff, sk-create-repo-rule, sk-create-frontmatter, sk-create-goal, sk-create-with-human-voice, sk-create-quality-control, human-voice-rules, hvr, shared-backbone, create-quality-control-pipeline -->\n11: \n12: # Documentation Authoring Hub (sk-doc)\n13: \n14: One advisor identity, fifteen workflow modes across fourteen packets, one shared sk-create-quality-control backbone.\n15: `sk-doc` is the parent hub for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring.\n16: It holds NO per-packet logic: it routes by `workflowMode` through `mode-registry.json`, and each packet keeps its own contract in its nested folder.\n17: The cross-cutting sk-create-quality-control pipeline (validators, global standards, frontmatter/llms/template assets) lives once in `shared/` and is consumed by every packet.\n18: \n19: **Version authority.** This file's `version` frontmatter is the hub's release version and matches the newest entry under `changelog/`; `description.json`, `mode-registry.json` and `hub-router.json` carry the same value, so every hub-root JSON routing file states the same release; `ROUTER.md` keeps its own document version.\n20: \n21: ---\n22: \n23: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n24: \n25: Use this skill for documentation and OpenCode-component authoring, and for document-quality work. The hub classifies the request, resolves a `workflowMode`, and loads the matching nested packet.\n26: \n27: | Mode | Use it for | Packet | Command |\n28: |------|------------|--------|---------|\n29: | **sk-create-skill** | Scaffold an OpenCode skill (and, via `sk-create-skill-parent`, a parent hub with nested mode packets) | `sk-create-skill/` | `/create:skill`, `/create:skill-parent` |\n30: | **sk-create-readme** | Author a folder README, general or code-folder | `sk-create-readme/` | `/create:readme` |\n31: | **sk-create-agent** | Scaffold an OpenCode agent (permission/authority frontmatter) | `sk-create-agent/` | `/create:agent` |\n32: | **sk-create-command** | Scaffold an OpenCode slash command (argument-hint + allowed-tools + router/presentation split) | `sk-create-command/` | `/create:command` |\n33: | **sk-create-feature-catalog** | Author a feature-catalog inventory package | `sk-create-feature-catalog/` | `/create:feature-catalog` |\n34: | **sk-create-manual-testing-playbook** | Author a manual-testing-playbook package | `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/` | `/create:manual-testing-playbook` |\n35: | **sk-create-benchmark** | Author MCP-promotion and behavior packages or inputs | `sk-create-benchmark/` | `/create:benchmark` |\n36: | **sk-create-changelog** | Author a global component changelog entry (version bump + topology-aware placement). Packet-local changelogs are deprecated | `sk-create-changelog/` | `/create:changelog` |\n37: | **sk-create-diff** | Produce a local, Git-free before/after review of an edited document (text/Markdown/HTML/DOCX/text-PDF) as a self-contained HTML report | `sk-create-diff/` | `/create:diff` |\n38: | **sk-create-repo-rule** | Create, revise or retire a repo-local rule under `repo-rules/` and wire it into `REPO RULES.md` (four decision tests refuse most requests) | `sk-create-repo-rule/` | `/create:repo-rule` |\n39: | **sk-create-with-human-voice** | Apply the Human Voice Rules to prose, or score prose against them, with a scope gate and a re-scan | `sk-create-with-human-voice/` | `/create:with-human-voice` |\n40: | **sk-create-frontmatter** | Own the YAML frontmatter contract: per-class field rules, the description budget, and the 4-part `version` derivation | `sk-create-frontmatter/` | — (routes via aliases) |\n41: | **sk-create-goal** | Author or revise a packet `goal.md`: the durable directive, three to seven self-contained completion criteria, and the phase-parent binding table (session-goal setting stays with the goal hooks) | `sk-create-goal/` | `/create:goal` |\n42: | **sk-create-quality-control** | Validate / score / optimize an EXISTING document (extract → DQI → HVR → validate) | `sk-create-quality-control/` | (routes via aliases) |\n43: \n44: ### When NOT to Use\n45: - Code implementation, tests, or debugging — use `sk-code`.\n46: - Git worktree, branch, commit, PR, or release work — use `sk-git`.\n47: - Spec-folder workflow / memory / save context — use `system-spec-kit`.\n48: - A measured Style Reference (design tokens extracted from a live source) — use `sk-design-md-generator`.\n49: - The `shared/` backbone is consumed by the packets, not invoked as a user workflow.\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n54: \n55: Routing is **registry-driven at runtime and authored in `hub-router.json`** in two stages.\n56: Stage 1 (hub → mode) selects a mode: `hub-router.json` `vocabularyClasses` is the one authored routing vocabulary.\n57: The `mode-registry.json` aliases, each packet's `Keyword triggers:` line and the root `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against it.\n58: `sk-create-skill/scripts/ci-hub-vocabulary-source.cjs` fails on the `alias`, `stage-two`, `triggers` and `packets` legs; its `stage-two-reverse` leg only reports.\n59: Stage 2 (mode → leaves) lives in the root [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md), which maps the request's authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources that mode loads.\n60: The two layers stay separate: the hub never emits leaf paths, and the surface router never re-decides the mode.\n61: The advisor routes every documentation and authoring query to the single identity `sk-doc`.\n62: Resolve the mode with `node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-doc --prompt \"<task>\"`.\n63: Act on the decision: `route` loads its `targets`, `clarify` and `defer` ask, `reject` refuses.\n64: Compiled routing is the default.\n65: An unclear documentation intent asks for disambiguation, because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n66: Run the compiled-routing directive in [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting hub resources.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n71: \n72: ### Terms\n73: \n74: - **`workflowMode`**: the public packet key a request resolves to, such as `sk-create-skill`.\n75: - **`packetKind`**: `workflow` for every sk-doc packet, because this hub has no surface axis.\n76: - **`backendKind`**: `template-scaffold` for the create-* generators and `sk-create-quality-control` for the quality-control mode.\n77: - **packet**: a nested `sk-create-*` folder that keeps its own authoring contract, references, assets and scripts.\n78: - **`mode-registry.json`**: the registry that owns each packet's discriminator, aliases, command and `advisorRouting`.\n79: - **`hub-router.json`**: stage one's signals, vocabulary classes, default fallback resource and bundle rules.\n80: - **`vocabularyClasses`**: the one authored routing vocabulary that the registry aliases, the `Keyword triggers:` lines and the `ROUTER.md` intents are checked against.\n81: - **`ROUTER.md`**: stage two's `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP`, which map an authoring intent to the packet-local leaf resources its mode loads.\n82: - **leaf**: a canonical `(workflowMode, leafResourceId)` pair from `leaf-manifest.json` that a `RESOURCE_MAP` path converts to.\n83: - **`defer`**: the outcome that asks for disambiguation, which this hub reaches because `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`.\n84: - **shared backbone**: the `shared/` copy of the sk-create-quality-control pipeline that every packet consumes.\n85: - **`graph-metadata.json`**: the one advisor identity for the whole skill, which no packet or `shared/` directory carries.\n86: \n87: ### Layout\n88: ```\n89: sk-doc/\n90:   SKILL.md               # this routing hub (no per-packet logic)\n91:   ROUTER.md              # stage-two surface router (authoring intent -> leaf sets)\n92:   mode-registry.json     # the fifteen-mode discriminator + advisorRouting (single source of truth)\n93:   hub-router.json        # router signals + vocabulary classes\n94:   description.json       # hub advisor descriptor\n95:   graph-metadata.json    # the ONE advisor identity for the whole skill\n96:   changelog/  manual-testing-playbook/  benchmark/  feature-catalog/\n97:   sk-create-skill/  sk-create-readme/  sk-create-agent/  sk-create-command/\n98:   sk-create-feature-catalog/  sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/\n99:   sk-create-benchmark/  sk-create-changelog/  sk-create-diff/\n100:   sk-create-repo-rule/  sk-create-frontmatter/  sk-create-goal/\n101:   sk-create-with-human-voice/  sk-create-quality-control/    # nested workflow packets\n102:   scripts/               # facade symlinks -> shared/ + owning packets (tool paths only)\n103:   shared/                # sk-create-quality-control backbone: validators, global standards, shared assets\n104: ```\n105: \n106: Each packet is self-contained (its own `SKILL.md`, `README.md`, `changelog/`, and moved `references/`/`assets/`/`scripts/`) and carries **no** `graph-metadata.json`, so the advisor discovers exactly one `sk-doc` identity.\n107: \n108: ### Shared backbone\n109: `shared/` holds the universal sk-create-quality-control pipeline consumed by every packet:\n110: - Generic validator scripts in `shared/scripts/`.\n111: - Cross-cutting standards and vocabulary in `shared/references/`.\n112: - Shared templates in `shared/assets/`.\n113: \n114: The generic validator `shared/scripts/cite-drift-scan.mjs` counts and resolves the `<path>.<ext>:<line>` citations in every tracked skill doc.\n115: It makes zero model calls by default.\n116: `--jev` runs the backend and needs `--out <dir>`, so every call is recorded, and the Jev arm and the advisory live in the sibling `shared/scripts/classifier-cite-drift-scan.mjs`.\n117: `validate_document.py` runs the same check as a non-blocking advisory on the document it validates.\n118: It prints `cite-drift advisory:` lines only when a Jev credential is stored, never changes the exit code, and `JEV_FEATURE_CITE_DRIFT=0` (older name: `SKDOC_CITE_DRIFT_CHECK=0`) skips it.\n119: The `sk-doc/scripts/` root directory keeps facade symlinks pointing inward to `shared/` and the owning packets so tool paths resolve.\n120: There are no hub-root `assets/` or `references/` aggregation directories: consumers reference each packet's own `assets/`/`references/` or the `shared/` backbone directly.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 4. RULES\n125: \n126: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n127: - **ALWAYS** resolve a packet through `mode-registry.json`; never hardcode a router mapping in the hub.\n128: - **ALWAYS** keep authoring contracts in the packets; the hub stays routing-only.\n129: - **ALWAYS** keep exactly one `graph-metadata.json` (this hub's) so the advisor sees one identity.\n130: - **ALWAYS** keep the root `ROUTER.md` `RESOURCE_MAP` in sync with `leaf-manifest.json` and `leaf-aliases.json` — the leaf sets dual-read to canonical typed pairs at the one contract boundary.\n131: - **ALWAYS** keep the sk-create-quality-control pipeline as one shared source under `shared/`, consumed by the packets.\n132: - **ALWAYS** keep changelogs as real files at the hub and in each packet — never symlinked.\n133: \n134: ### ⛔ NEVER\n135: - **NEVER** add a `graph-metadata.json` inside a packet or `shared/`.\n136: - **NEVER** put per-packet authoring logic in the hub.\n137: - **NEVER** add a surface axis or a `surfaceBundle` outcome — sk-doc is workflow-only.\n138: \n139: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n140: - A new documentation workflow is needed — extend `mode-registry.json` and open a packet; do not bolt logic onto the hub.\n141: - A packet would become a near-empty shell (generic doctrine, no type-specific behavior) — fold it into `shared/` instead.\n142: \n143: ---\n144: \n145: ## 5. REFERENCES\n146: \n147: - Registry: `mode-registry.json` (fifteen modes across fourteen packets, `packetKind: workflow`).\n148: - Hub router: `hub-router.json` (signals + vocabulary classes).\n149: - Surface router: `ROUTER.md` (authoring intent to packet-local leaf sets).\n150: - Advisor descriptor: `description.json`; skill-graph identity: `graph-metadata.json`.\n151: - Packets: `sk-create-skill/`, `sk-create-readme/`, `sk-create-agent/`, `sk-create-command/`, `sk-create-feature-catalog/`, `sk-create-manual-testing-playbook/`, `sk-create-benchmark/`, `sk-create-changelog/`, `sk-create-diff/`, `sk-create-repo-rule/`, `sk-create-frontmatter/`, `sk-create-goal/`, `sk-create-with-human-voice/`, `sk-create-quality-control/`.\n152: - Shared backbone: `shared/scripts/`, `shared/references/`, `shared/assets/`.\n153: - Parent-skill pattern: `sk-create-skill/references/parent-skill/parent-skills-nested-packets.md`."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546529848,"end":1794546530132}}}}
